ation and changed its name to Murphy Oil Corporation in 1964. The company was incorporated in 1950 and is headquartered in Houston, Texas.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Eric M. Hambly | President, CEO & Director | Age 50 | Compensation: $2.3M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 813 people. Revenue per employee of $3.3M ranks in the above the sector median among Energy peers, with revenue falling 11%/yr across its filed history. Each employee generates $279K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $62K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 1\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags moderate compensation governance risk (4/10).","financial_summary":["Murphy Oil Corporation is a mid-cap energy business with a $4.6B market cap on $2.7B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at -12% over the past three years and -1% annually across the full decade, and a shrinking top line is the hardest headwind any business can overcome.","Returns on capital run at 8% against a 9% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests.","The business throws off $227M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 8 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 11.97\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, leverage is comfortably conservative at 0.8\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and interest coverage runs at 3.1\u00d7.","Management returns a meaningful 6.3% of market cap to owners through dividends and buybacks, a strong signal of disciplined capital allocation, and insiders hold 5.7% of the shares."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Piotroski score 4/9 \u2014 below the median for a healthy business","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Altman Z in the grey zone \u2014 financial health warrants monitoring","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Above-average short interest at 9.7% of float","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Revenue growth lags most Energy peers \u2014 losing share inside the profit pool","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Cyclical sectors like Energy tend to underperform in slowdowns","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"High market volatility with VIX at 36, signaling elevated fear","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"Widening credit spreads suggest growing stress in corporate debt markets","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"Geopolitical supply shocks \u2014 wars, sanctions, and OPEC decisions create volatility that no hedging programme can fully neutralise.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Valued at a 49% discount to sector median EV/EBITDA","42 consecutive years of dividend payments \u2014 an operating record that makes the dividend claim credible","Healthy payout ratio leaves room for both dividend growth and reinvestment","Energy sector showing strong relative momentum, outperforming the market by 8% over 3 months"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"Murphy Oil (NYSE:MUR) Delivers A Strong Quarter: What\u2019s Next?","source":"kalkinemedia.com","link":"https://kalkinemedia.com/us/stocks/energy/murphy-oil-nysemur-delivers-a-strong-quarter-whats-next","date":"2026-11-20","timestamp":1795184760.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.807,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":["earnings"]},{"title":"(MUR) Price Dynamics and Execution-Aware Positioning","source":"news.stocktradersdaily.com","link":"https://news.stocktradersdaily.com/news_release/38/MUR_Price_Dynamics_and_Execution-Aware_Positioning_111926071001_1795090201.html","date":"2026-11-19","timestamp":1795090201.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.494,"tier":0,"scope":"comp